  "id": "anti-rotation",
  "name": "Anti-Rotation",
  "definition": "A strict unilateral isometric trunk family in which a horizontal band anchored to one side tends to rotate the spine and pelvis while the athlete maintains a feet-together standing posture with both arms extended at shoulder height. The initial fixture is a hold rather than a dynamic press: spine.rotation is the external tendency being resisted and no deliberate shoulder, elbow, spinal, pelvic, hip, knee, or ankle motion creates a repetition. The direct exercise study measured sacral acceleration rather than EMG and deliberately varied the lower-body base of support. Obliques are primary through the reviewed self-opposed rotation semantics and anatomy; abs, lumbarExtensors, serratus, anterior deltoid, triceps, finger flexors, extensor carpi radialis, gluteus medius, vasti, and soleus are conservative anatomy-and-mechanics-derived stabilizers of the materially controlled upper- and lower-body chain, not study-measured Pallof roles. Extensor carpi radialis supplies neutral-wrist counter-control against the finger flexors' wrist-flexion moment during the static handle task. The visible oblique region cannot encode internal versus external or left versus right recruitment. Cable-stack, dynamic-press, kneeling, split-stance, tandem, unstable-surface, overhead, chopping, and hybrid variants remain outside this contract.",
  "evidenceRefs": [
    "christophy-2012-lumbar-spine",
    "ackland-2008-shoulder-moment-arms",
    "arnold-2010-lower-limb",
    "holzbaur-2005-upper-extremity",
    "seth-2019-shoulder-work",
    "juan-recio-2025-pallof-postural-challenge",
    "cinarli-2025-anti-movement-training"
  ],
